# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-subjection-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: mill-subjection-1.logic.r1 has "discrepancy ∧ opposed ∧ swept"; mill-subjection-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-subjection-1.logic.r1.3 have "swept" and "discrepancy ∧ opposed → reflection"; they differ when only "there is an entire discrepancy between this one social fact and all those which accompany it", "its nature is radically opposed to the progressive movement of the modern world" and "the progressive movement has successively swept away everything else of an analogous character" are true, which the first allows
mill-subjection-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps mill-subjection-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-subjection-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "there is an entire discrepancy between this one social fact and all those which accompany it", "its nature is radically opposed to the progressive movement of the modern world" and "the progressive movement has successively swept away everything else of an analogous character" are true and "this affords a conscientious observer serious matter for reflection" is false. Is S2, "This entire discrepancy between one social fact and all those which accompany it, and the radical opposition between its nature and the progressive movement which is the boast of the modern world, and which has successively swept away everything else of an analogous character, surely affords, to a conscientious observer of human tendencies, serious matter for reflection.", true then?
mill-subjection-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 3 ways: mill-subjection-1.logic.r1 has nothing from it; mill-subjection-1.logic.r1.2 has "discrepancy ∧ opposed → presumption" and "presumption → balanced"; they differ when only "there is an entire discrepancy between this one social fact and all those which accompany it", "its nature is radically opposed to the progressive movement of the modern world" and "a prima facie presumption is raised on the unfavourable side, outweighing any which custom and usage could create on the favourable" are true, which the first allows; mill-subjection-1.logic.r1.3 has "discrepancy ∧ opposed ∧ swept → presumption" and "presumption → balanced"
mill-subjection-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps mill-subjection-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-subjection-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "there is an entire discrepancy between this one social fact and all those which accompany it", "its nature is radically opposed to the progressive movement of the modern world", "the progressive movement has successively swept away everything else of an analogous character" and "a prima facie presumption is raised on the unfavourable side, outweighing any which custom and usage could create on the favourable" are true and "the question of women's subordination is at least a balanced question" is false. Is S3, "It raises a prima facie presumption on the unfavourable side, far outweighing any which custom and usage could in such circumstances create on the favourable; and should at least suffice to make this, like the choice between republicanism and royalty, a balanced question.", true then?
mill-subjection-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: mill-subjection-1.logic.r1 has "isolated → discrepancy" and "discrepancy ∧ opposed ∧ swept → presumption" and "presumption → balanced"; mill-subjection-1.logic.r1.2 has "isolated → discrepancy" and "relic ∧ swept → opposed"; they differ when only "the social subordination of women is an isolated fact in modern social institutions, a solitary breach of their fundamental law", "the social subordination of women is a relic of an old world of thought and practice exploded in everything else", "there is an entire discrepancy between this one social fact and all those which accompany it", "the progressive movement has successively swept away everything else of an analogous character", "a prima facie presumption is raised on the unfavourable side, outweighing any which custom and usage could create on the favourable" and "the question of women's subordination is at least a balanced question" are true, which the first allows; mill-subjection-1.logic.r1.3 has "isolated ∧ relic → discrepancy ∧ opposed"
